[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [[:db/retract 17592186323338 :answer-field/choices 17592186322742] [:db/retract 17592186323338 :answer-field/choices 17592186321673] [:db/retract 17592186323338 :answer-field/choices 17592186321630] [:db/retract 17592186323338 :answer-field/choices 17592186322623] [:db/retract 17592186335365 :answer-field/choices 17592186333364] [:db/retract 17592186335365 :answer-field/choices 17592186333924] [:db/retract 17592186335365 :answer-field/choices 17592186334552] [:db/retract 17592186335365 :answer-field/choices 17592186334888] [:db/retract 17592186335365 :answer-field/choices 17592186334591] [:db/retract 17592186338328 :answer-field/choices 17592186336837] [:db/retract 17592186338328 :answer-field/choices 17592186337733] [:db/retract 17592186338328 :answer-field/choices 17592186338151] [:db/retract 17592186338328 :answer-field/choices 17592186336201] [:db/retract 17592186338328 :answer-field/choices 17592186338122] [:db/retract 17592186381042 :answer-field/choices 17592186381043] [:db/retract 17592186381042 :answer-field/choices 17592186381044] [:db/retract 17592186381042 :answer-field/choices 17592186381045] [:db/retract 17592186381042 :answer-field/choices 17592186381046] [:db/retract 17592186381042 :answer-field/choices 17592186381047] {:db/id 17592186323840 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2} & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}>0\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2} & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}>0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186323338 :answer-field/correct "answer-7bf85391-7038-5476-a5a6-9137fe1a1550" :answer-field/choices ["answer-36305481-9e3d-5e25-954d-d2f0ee7b3a0e" "answer-7bf85391-7038-5476-a5a6-9137fe1a1550" "answer-57cb5bef-8f7f-5b7a-a338-9773d515202d" "answer-9e370f5c-fe89-5059-826d-f674e7536738"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-36305481-9e3d-5e25-954d-d2f0ee7b3a0e" :answer/id #uuid "36305481-9e3d-5e25-954d-d2f0ee7b3a0e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}<0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7bf85391-7038-5476-a5a6-9137fe1a1550" :answer/id #uuid "7bf85391-7038-5476-a5a6-9137fe1a1550" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}>0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-57cb5bef-8f7f-5b7a-a338-9773d515202d" :answer/id #uuid "57cb5bef-8f7f-5b7a-a338-9773d515202d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\ge 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9e370f5c-fe89-5059-826d-f674e7536738" :answer/id #uuid "9e370f5c-fe89-5059-826d-f674e7536738" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\le 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125193 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$ is defined only when $xy>0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}(x,y)=1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$ is defined only when $xy>0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${f}_{y}(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186335365 :answer-field/correct "answer-2f872de4-cb47-5592-8dc7-6e0b4870f5ce" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5679c97e-3be4-5e8f-9f0e-adf8640a6566" "answer-2f872de4-cb47-5592-8dc7-6e0b4870f5ce" "answer-e793c2a0-49a5-5b1b-8d1e-aca8ba275497" "answer-085d0423-3736-55fb-8127-75744ca06c74" "answer-6ab0671e-fe7c-567e-b9ec-22673c0fe32f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5679c97e-3be4-5e8f-9f0e-adf8640a6566" :answer/id #uuid "5679c97e-3be4-5e8f-9f0e-adf8640a6566" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy<0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f872de4-cb47-5592-8dc7-6e0b4870f5ce" :answer/id #uuid "2f872de4-cb47-5592-8dc7-6e0b4870f5ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e793c2a0-49a5-5b1b-8d1e-aca8ba275497" :answer/id #uuid "e793c2a0-49a5-5b1b-8d1e-aca8ba275497" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy\\ge 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-085d0423-3736-55fb-8127-75744ca06c74" :answer/id #uuid "085d0423-3736-55fb-8127-75744ca06c74" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ab0671e-fe7c-567e-b9ec-22673c0fe32f" :answer/id #uuid "6ab0671e-fe7c-567e-b9ec-22673c0fe32f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186339041 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z>0\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+y+z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{z}(x,y,z)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{z}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $z$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z>0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186338328 :answer-field/correct "answer-e076e87f-9d46-5e8e-aae7-3008bc1bca80" :answer-field/choices ["answer-08126234-d582-5c31-b848-f06a0d4f509f" "answer-e076e87f-9d46-5e8e-aae7-3008bc1bca80" "answer-26b5f509-12c5-56ec-a821-c5fafb2ece8f" "answer-76653d66-41fe-56fb-8ad2-97bff7e4602b" "answer-c55241b6-baf2-5bb2-8899-9699eab4a0e2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-08126234-d582-5c31-b848-f06a0d4f509f" :answer/id #uuid "08126234-d582-5c31-b848-f06a0d4f509f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z=0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e076e87f-9d46-5e8e-aae7-3008bc1bca80" :answer/id #uuid "e076e87f-9d46-5e8e-aae7-3008bc1bca80" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z>0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26b5f509-12c5-56ec-a821-c5fafb2ece8f" :answer/id #uuid "26b5f509-12c5-56ec-a821-c5fafb2ece8f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z\\ge 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-76653d66-41fe-56fb-8ad2-97bff7e4602b" :answer/id #uuid "76653d66-41fe-56fb-8ad2-97bff7e4602b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z\\le 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c55241b6-baf2-5bb2-8899-9699eab4a0e2" :answer/id #uuid "c55241b6-baf2-5bb2-8899-9699eab4a0e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186381041 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & >0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,x+y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)}{x+y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+y}\\cdot \\frac{1}{z} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{z(x+y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{z}(x,y,z)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z(x+y) & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{z}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,x+y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $z$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,x+y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,x+y\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,x+y\\ne 0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186381042 :answer-field/correct "answer-b052ba04-365f-5ed1-957c-0f2f21bb0450" :answer-field/choices ["answer-584528bc-1e7a-5d0a-bc25-914c0e983cf8" "answer-aba8a0b5-f592-5ef2-9593-8ac110a070d0" "answer-b052ba04-365f-5ed1-957c-0f2f21bb0450" "answer-6e0c14b9-a4f1-5a39-a51f-050ad0557e49" "answer-82a39c5f-bdf3-5e97-a763-c39be177a14d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-584528bc-1e7a-5d0a-bc25-914c0e983cf8" :answer/id #uuid "584528bc-1e7a-5d0a-bc25-914c0e983cf8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aba8a0b5-f592-5ef2-9593-8ac110a070d0" :answer/id #uuid "aba8a0b5-f592-5ef2-9593-8ac110a070d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,x+y>0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b052ba04-365f-5ed1-957c-0f2f21bb0450" :answer/id #uuid "b052ba04-365f-5ed1-957c-0f2f21bb0450" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,x+y\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e0c14b9-a4f1-5a39-a51f-050ad0557e49" :answer/id #uuid "6e0c14b9-a4f1-5a39-a51f-050ad0557e49" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z>0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82a39c5f-bdf3-5e97-a763-c39be177a14d" :answer/id #uuid "82a39c5f-bdf3-5e97-a763-c39be177a14d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,x+y\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124466 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-244be0b4-aca1-5c1c-abe2-94099e0fbbe5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-244be0b4-aca1-5c1c-abe2-94099e0fbbe5" :question/id #uuid "244be0b4-aca1-5c1c-abe2-94099e0fbbe5" :question/math-academy-id "q-132751" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{z+y-{x}^{2}}?$\n\nI. The partial derivative with respect to $x$ is defined at $(0,0,1)$\n\nII. ${f}_{y}(1,2,0)$ is defined\n\nIII. ${f}_{z}(1,1,0)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{z+y-{x}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z+y-{x}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z+y-{x}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(0,0,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,0,1)=\\sqrt{1+0-{0}^{2}}=1,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{x}$ is also defined at $(0,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,0,1) & =-\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{1+0-{0}^{2}}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,2,0)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,2,0)=\\sqrt{0+2-{1}^{2}}=1,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(1,2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,2,0) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{0+2-{1}^{2}}}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $(1,1,0)$ into ${f}_{z}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{z}(1,1,0)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{0+1-{1}^{2}}}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only'." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-73207714-b850-5ffd-8c57-6fa10d88caae"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-73207714-b850-5ffd-8c57-6fa10d88caae" :answer-field/id #uuid "73207714-b850-5ffd-8c57-6fa10d88caae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6b1e694a-1db7-5721-b7ce-8435d79a5611" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6b1e694a-1db7-5721-b7ce-8435d79a5611" "answer-dcde3ca7-4fbb-5c0b-bafc-57f55ce518b8" "answer-dfc647e1-2b5b-579f-92cf-20efc3da4346" "answer-8fd0ef33-4fca-54c2-829e-50a7815c63c8" "answer-faf5e9ca-69be-5548-8107-7d06c638adcc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b1e694a-1db7-5721-b7ce-8435d79a5611" :answer/id #uuid "6b1e694a-1db7-5721-b7ce-8435d79a5611" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dcde3ca7-4fbb-5c0b-bafc-57f55ce518b8" :answer/id #uuid "dcde3ca7-4fbb-5c0b-bafc-57f55ce518b8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dfc647e1-2b5b-579f-92cf-20efc3da4346" :answer/id #uuid "dfc647e1-2b5b-579f-92cf-20efc3da4346" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8fd0ef33-4fca-54c2-829e-50a7815c63c8" :answer/id #uuid "8fd0ef33-4fca-54c2-829e-50a7815c63c8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-faf5e9ca-69be-5548-8107-7d06c638adcc" :answer/id #uuid "faf5e9ca-69be-5548-8107-7d06c638adcc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-062eec2e-80dd-50ca-8380-634dda28eed3" :activity/id #uuid "062eec2e-80dd-50ca-8380-634dda28eed3" :activity/title "Partial Differentiability of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048877 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-2842369f-1f4f-58be-953d-3f713b247f9b" "step-6bba2763-669c-58c3-a3e6-5843dc68f94f" "step-ad4fbff0-775d-5cc6-a06c-c04820df7660" "step-a90d8bfb-8461-5668-8e11-2111cb68de7c" "step-33af0b6f-ae95-578f-99a9-1e3131763eb2"] :activity/first-step "step-2842369f-1f4f-58be-953d-3f713b247f9b" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-2842369f-1f4f-58be-953d-3f713b247f9b" :step/id #uuid "2842369f-1f4f-58be-953d-3f713b247f9b" :step/content 17592186323840 :step/next "step-6bba2763-669c-58c3-a3e6-5843dc68f94f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-6bba2763-669c-58c3-a3e6-5843dc68f94f" :step/id #uuid "6bba2763-669c-58c3-a3e6-5843dc68f94f" :step/content 17592186125193 :step/next "step-ad4fbff0-775d-5cc6-a06c-c04820df7660" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ad4fbff0-775d-5cc6-a06c-c04820df7660" :step/id #uuid "ad4fbff0-775d-5cc6-a06c-c04820df7660" :step/content 17592186339041 :step/next "step-a90d8bfb-8461-5668-8e11-2111cb68de7c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a90d8bfb-8461-5668-8e11-2111cb68de7c" :step/id #uuid "a90d8bfb-8461-5668-8e11-2111cb68de7c" :step/content 17592186381041 :step/next "step-33af0b6f-ae95-578f-99a9-1e3131763eb2" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-33af0b6f-ae95-578f-99a9-1e3131763eb2" :step/id #uuid "33af0b6f-ae95-578f-99a9-1e3131763eb2" :step/content "question-244be0b4-aca1-5c1c-abe2-94099e0fbbe5" :db/ensure [:step/validate]}] "assertions" [[17592186323840 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2} & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}>0\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2} & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}>0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] ["answer-36305481-9e3d-5e25-954d-d2f0ee7b3a0e" :answer/id #uuid "36305481-9e3d-5e25-954d-d2f0ee7b3a0e"] ["answer-36305481-9e3d-5e25-954d-d2f0ee7b3a0e" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-36305481-9e3d-5e25-954d-d2f0ee7b3a0e" :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}<0\\}"] ["answer-7bf85391-7038-5476-a5a6-9137fe1a1550" :answer/id #uuid "7bf85391-7038-5476-a5a6-9137fe1a1550"] ["answer-7bf85391-7038-5476-a5a6-9137fe1a1550" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-7bf85391-7038-5476-a5a6-9137fe1a1550" :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}>0\\}"] ["answer-57cb5bef-8f7f-5b7a-a338-9773d515202d" :answer/id #uuid "57cb5bef-8f7f-5b7a-a338-9773d515202d"] ["answer-57cb5bef-8f7f-5b7a-a338-9773d515202d" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-57cb5bef-8f7f-5b7a-a338-9773d515202d" :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\ge 0\\}"] ["answer-9e370f5c-fe89-5059-826d-f674e7536738" :answer/id #uuid "9e370f5c-fe89-5059-826d-f674e7536738"] ["answer-9e370f5c-fe89-5059-826d-f674e7536738" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-9e370f5c-fe89-5059-826d-f674e7536738" :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\le 0\\}"] [17592186323338 :answer-field/correct "answer-7bf85391-7038-5476-a5a6-9137fe1a1550"] [17592186323338 :answer-field/choices "answer-36305481-9e3d-5e25-954d-d2f0ee7b3a0e"] [17592186323338 :answer-field/choices "answer-7bf85391-7038-5476-a5a6-9137fe1a1550"] [17592186323338 :answer-field/choices "answer-57cb5bef-8f7f-5b7a-a338-9773d515202d"] [17592186323338 :answer-field/choices "answer-9e370f5c-fe89-5059-826d-f674e7536738"] [17592186125193 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$ is defined only when $xy>0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}(x,y)=1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$ is defined only when $xy>0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${f}_{y}(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] ["answer-5679c97e-3be4-5e8f-9f0e-adf8640a6566" :answer/id #uuid "5679c97e-3be4-5e8f-9f0e-adf8640a6566"] ["answer-5679c97e-3be4-5e8f-9f0e-adf8640a6566" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-5679c97e-3be4-5e8f-9f0e-adf8640a6566" :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy<0\\}"] ["answer-2f872de4-cb47-5592-8dc7-6e0b4870f5ce" :answer/id #uuid "2f872de4-cb47-5592-8dc7-6e0b4870f5ce"] ["answer-2f872de4-cb47-5592-8dc7-6e0b4870f5ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-2f872de4-cb47-5592-8dc7-6e0b4870f5ce" :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}"] ["answer-e793c2a0-49a5-5b1b-8d1e-aca8ba275497" :answer/id #uuid "e793c2a0-49a5-5b1b-8d1e-aca8ba275497"] ["answer-e793c2a0-49a5-5b1b-8d1e-aca8ba275497" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-e793c2a0-49a5-5b1b-8d1e-aca8ba275497" :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy\\ge 0\\}"] ["answer-085d0423-3736-55fb-8127-75744ca06c74" :answer/id #uuid "085d0423-3736-55fb-8127-75744ca06c74"] ["answer-085d0423-3736-55fb-8127-75744ca06c74" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-085d0423-3736-55fb-8127-75744ca06c74" :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0\\}"] ["answer-6ab0671e-fe7c-567e-b9ec-22673c0fe32f" :answer/id #uuid "6ab0671e-fe7c-567e-b9ec-22673c0fe32f"] ["answer-6ab0671e-fe7c-567e-b9ec-22673c0fe32f" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-6ab0671e-fe7c-567e-b9ec-22673c0fe32f" :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}"] [17592186335365 :answer-field/correct "answer-2f872de4-cb47-5592-8dc7-6e0b4870f5ce"] [17592186335365 :answer-field/choices "answer-5679c97e-3be4-5e8f-9f0e-adf8640a6566"] [17592186335365 :answer-field/choices "answer-2f872de4-cb47-5592-8dc7-6e0b4870f5ce"] [17592186335365 :answer-field/choices "answer-e793c2a0-49a5-5b1b-8d1e-aca8ba275497"] [17592186335365 :answer-field/choices "answer-085d0423-3736-55fb-8127-75744ca06c74"] [17592186335365 :answer-field/choices "answer-6ab0671e-fe7c-567e-b9ec-22673c0fe32f"] [17592186339041 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z>0\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+y+z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{z}(x,y,z)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{z}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $z$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z>0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] ["answer-08126234-d582-5c31-b848-f06a0d4f509f" :answer/id #uuid "08126234-d582-5c31-b848-f06a0d4f509f"] ["answer-08126234-d582-5c31-b848-f06a0d4f509f" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-08126234-d582-5c31-b848-f06a0d4f509f" :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z=0\\}"] ["answer-e076e87f-9d46-5e8e-aae7-3008bc1bca80" :answer/id #uuid "e076e87f-9d46-5e8e-aae7-3008bc1bca80"] ["answer-e076e87f-9d46-5e8e-aae7-3008bc1bca80" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-e076e87f-9d46-5e8e-aae7-3008bc1bca80" :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z>0\\}"] ["answer-26b5f509-12c5-56ec-a821-c5fafb2ece8f" :answer/id #uuid "26b5f509-12c5-56ec-a821-c5fafb2ece8f"] ["answer-26b5f509-12c5-56ec-a821-c5fafb2ece8f" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-26b5f509-12c5-56ec-a821-c5fafb2ece8f" :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z\\ge 0\\}"] ["answer-76653d66-41fe-56fb-8ad2-97bff7e4602b" :answer/id #uuid "76653d66-41fe-56fb-8ad2-97bff7e4602b"] ["answer-76653d66-41fe-56fb-8ad2-97bff7e4602b" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-76653d66-41fe-56fb-8ad2-97bff7e4602b" :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z\\le 0\\}"] ["answer-c55241b6-baf2-5bb2-8899-9699eab4a0e2" :answer/id #uuid "c55241b6-baf2-5bb2-8899-9699eab4a0e2"] ["answer-c55241b6-baf2-5bb2-8899-9699eab4a0e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-c55241b6-baf2-5bb2-8899-9699eab4a0e2" :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z\\ne 0\\}"] [17592186338328 :answer-field/correct "answer-e076e87f-9d46-5e8e-aae7-3008bc1bca80"] [17592186338328 :answer-field/choices "answer-08126234-d582-5c31-b848-f06a0d4f509f"] [17592186338328 :answer-field/choices "answer-e076e87f-9d46-5e8e-aae7-3008bc1bca80"] [17592186338328 :answer-field/choices "answer-26b5f509-12c5-56ec-a821-c5fafb2ece8f"] [17592186338328 :answer-field/choices "answer-76653d66-41fe-56fb-8ad2-97bff7e4602b"] [17592186338328 :answer-field/choices "answer-c55241b6-baf2-5bb2-8899-9699eab4a0e2"] [17592186381041 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & >0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,x+y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)}{x+y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+y}\\cdot \\frac{1}{z} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{z(x+y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{z}(x,y,z)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z(x+y) & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{z}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,x+y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $z$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,x+y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,x+y\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,x+y\\ne 0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] ["answer-584528bc-1e7a-5d0a-bc25-914c0e983cf8" :answer/id #uuid "584528bc-1e7a-5d0a-bc25-914c0e983cf8"] ["answer-584528bc-1e7a-5d0a-bc25-914c0e983cf8" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-584528bc-1e7a-5d0a-bc25-914c0e983cf8" :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y\\ne 0\\}"] ["answer-aba8a0b5-f592-5ef2-9593-8ac110a070d0" :answer/id #uuid "aba8a0b5-f592-5ef2-9593-8ac110a070d0"] ["answer-aba8a0b5-f592-5ef2-9593-8ac110a070d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-aba8a0b5-f592-5ef2-9593-8ac110a070d0" :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,x+y>0\\}"] ["answer-b052ba04-365f-5ed1-957c-0f2f21bb0450" :answer/id #uuid "b052ba04-365f-5ed1-957c-0f2f21bb0450"] ["answer-b052ba04-365f-5ed1-957c-0f2f21bb0450" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-b052ba04-365f-5ed1-957c-0f2f21bb0450" :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,x+y\\ne 0\\}"] ["answer-6e0c14b9-a4f1-5a39-a51f-050ad0557e49" :answer/id #uuid "6e0c14b9-a4f1-5a39-a51f-050ad0557e49"] ["answer-6e0c14b9-a4f1-5a39-a51f-050ad0557e49" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-6e0c14b9-a4f1-5a39-a51f-050ad0557e49" :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z>0\\}"] ["answer-82a39c5f-bdf3-5e97-a763-c39be177a14d" :answer/id #uuid "82a39c5f-bdf3-5e97-a763-c39be177a14d"] ["answer-82a39c5f-bdf3-5e97-a763-c39be177a14d" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-82a39c5f-bdf3-5e97-a763-c39be177a14d" :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,x+y\\ne 0\\}"] [17592186381042 :answer-field/correct "answer-b052ba04-365f-5ed1-957c-0f2f21bb0450"] [17592186381042 :answer-field/choices "answer-584528bc-1e7a-5d0a-bc25-914c0e983cf8"] [17592186381042 :answer-field/choices "answer-aba8a0b5-f592-5ef2-9593-8ac110a070d0"] [17592186381042 :answer-field/choices "answer-b052ba04-365f-5ed1-957c-0f2f21bb0450"] [17592186381042 :answer-field/choices "answer-6e0c14b9-a4f1-5a39-a51f-050ad0557e49"] [17592186381042 :answer-field/choices "answer-82a39c5f-bdf3-5e97-a763-c39be177a14d"] ["question-244be0b4-aca1-5c1c-abe2-94099e0fbbe5" :question/id #uuid "244be0b4-aca1-5c1c-abe2-94099e0fbbe5"] ["question-244be0b4-aca1-5c1c-abe2-94099e0fbbe5" :question/math-academy-id "q-132751"] ["question-244be0b4-aca1-5c1c-abe2-94099e0fbbe5" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{z+y-{x}^{2}}?$\n\nI. The partial derivative with respect to $x$ is defined at $(0,0,1)$\n\nII. ${f}_{y}(1,2,0)$ is defined\n\nIII. ${f}_{z}(1,1,0)$ is defined"] ["question-244be0b4-aca1-5c1c-abe2-94099e0fbbe5" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{z+y-{x}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z+y-{x}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z+y-{x}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(0,0,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,0,1)=\\sqrt{1+0-{0}^{2}}=1,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{x}$ is also defined at $(0,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,0,1) & =-\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{1+0-{0}^{2}}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,2,0)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,2,0)=\\sqrt{0+2-{1}^{2}}=1,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(1,2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,2,0) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{0+2-{1}^{2}}}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $(1,1,0)$ into ${f}_{z}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{z}(1,1,0)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{0+1-{1}^{2}}}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only'."] ["question-244be0b4-aca1-5c1c-abe2-94099e0fbbe5" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard] ["answer-field-73207714-b850-5ffd-8c57-6fa10d88caae" :answer-field/id #uuid "73207714-b850-5ffd-8c57-6fa10d88caae"] ["answer-6b1e694a-1db7-5721-b7ce-8435d79a5611" :answer/id #uuid "6b1e694a-1db7-5721-b7ce-8435d79a5611"] ["answer-6b1e694a-1db7-5721-b7ce-8435d79a5611" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-6b1e694a-1db7-5721-b7ce-8435d79a5611" :answer/value "I and II only"] ["answer-dcde3ca7-4fbb-5c0b-bafc-57f55ce518b8" :answer/id #uuid "dcde3ca7-4fbb-5c0b-bafc-57f55ce518b8"] ["answer-dcde3ca7-4fbb-5c0b-bafc-57f55ce518b8" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-dcde3ca7-4fbb-5c0b-bafc-57f55ce518b8" :answer/value "I and III only"] ["answer-dfc647e1-2b5b-579f-92cf-20efc3da4346" :answer/id #uuid "dfc647e1-2b5b-579f-92cf-20efc3da4346"] ["answer-dfc647e1-2b5b-579f-92cf-20efc3da4346" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-dfc647e1-2b5b-579f-92cf-20efc3da4346" :answer/value "I only"] ["answer-8fd0ef33-4fca-54c2-829e-50a7815c63c8" :answer/id #uuid "8fd0ef33-4fca-54c2-829e-50a7815c63c8"] ["answer-8fd0ef33-4fca-54c2-829e-50a7815c63c8" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-8fd0ef33-4fca-54c2-829e-50a7815c63c8" :answer/value "II and III only"] ["answer-faf5e9ca-69be-5548-8107-7d06c638adcc" :answer/id #uuid "faf5e9ca-69be-5548-8107-7d06c638adcc"] ["answer-faf5e9ca-69be-5548-8107-7d06c638adcc" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-faf5e9ca-69be-5548-8107-7d06c638adcc" :answer/value "II only"] ["answer-field-73207714-b850-5ffd-8c57-6fa10d88caae" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-73207714-b850-5ffd-8c57-6fa10d88caae" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-73207714-b850-5ffd-8c57-6fa10d88caae" :answer-field/correct "answer-6b1e694a-1db7-5721-b7ce-8435d79a5611"] ["answer-field-73207714-b850-5ffd-8c57-6fa10d88caae" :answer-field/choices "answer-6b1e694a-1db7-5721-b7ce-8435d79a5611"] ["answer-field-73207714-b850-5ffd-8c57-6fa10d88caae" :answer-field/choices "answer-dcde3ca7-4fbb-5c0b-bafc-57f55ce518b8"] ["answer-field-73207714-b850-5ffd-8c57-6fa10d88caae" :answer-field/choices "answer-dfc647e1-2b5b-579f-92cf-20efc3da4346"] ["answer-field-73207714-b850-5ffd-8c57-6fa10d88caae" :answer-field/choices "answer-8fd0ef33-4fca-54c2-829e-50a7815c63c8"] ["answer-field-73207714-b850-5ffd-8c57-6fa10d88caae" :answer-field/choices "answer-faf5e9ca-69be-5548-8107-7d06c638adcc"] ["question-244be0b4-aca1-5c1c-abe2-94099e0fbbe5" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-73207714-b850-5ffd-8c57-6fa10d88caae"] [17592186124466 :knowledge-point/questions "question-244be0b4-aca1-5c1c-abe2-94099e0fbbe5"] ["activity-062eec2e-80dd-50ca-8380-634dda28eed3" :activity/id #uuid "062eec2e-80dd-50ca-8380-634dda28eed3"] ["step-2842369f-1f4f-58be-953d-3f713b247f9b" :step/id #uuid "2842369f-1f4f-58be-953d-3f713b247f9b"] ["step-6bba2763-669c-58c3-a3e6-5843dc68f94f" :step/id #uuid "6bba2763-669c-58c3-a3e6-5843dc68f94f"] ["step-ad4fbff0-775d-5cc6-a06c-c04820df7660" :step/id #uuid "ad4fbff0-775d-5cc6-a06c-c04820df7660"] ["step-a90d8bfb-8461-5668-8e11-2111cb68de7c" :step/id #uuid "a90d8bfb-8461-5668-8e11-2111cb68de7c"] ["step-33af0b6f-ae95-578f-99a9-1e3131763eb2" :step/id #uuid "33af0b6f-ae95-578f-99a9-1e3131763eb2"] ["step-2842369f-1f4f-58be-953d-3f713b247f9b" :step/content 17592186323840] ["step-2842369f-1f4f-58be-953d-3f713b247f9b" :step/next "step-6bba2763-669c-58c3-a3e6-5843dc68f94f"] ["step-6bba2763-669c-58c3-a3e6-5843dc68f94f" :step/content 17592186125193] ["step-6bba2763-669c-58c3-a3e6-5843dc68f94f" :step/next "step-ad4fbff0-775d-5cc6-a06c-c04820df7660"] ["step-ad4fbff0-775d-5cc6-a06c-c04820df7660" :step/content 17592186339041] ["step-ad4fbff0-775d-5cc6-a06c-c04820df7660" :step/next "step-a90d8bfb-8461-5668-8e11-2111cb68de7c"] ["step-a90d8bfb-8461-5668-8e11-2111cb68de7c" :step/content 17592186381041] ["step-a90d8bfb-8461-5668-8e11-2111cb68de7c" :step/next "step-33af0b6f-ae95-578f-99a9-1e3131763eb2"] ["step-33af0b6f-ae95-578f-99a9-1e3131763eb2" :step/content "question-244be0b4-aca1-5c1c-abe2-94099e0fbbe5"] ["activity-062eec2e-80dd-50ca-8380-634dda28eed3" :activity/title "Partial Differentiability of Multivariable Functions"] ["activity-062eec2e-80dd-50ca-8380-634dda28eed3" :activity/type :activity.type/review] ["activity-062eec2e-80dd-50ca-8380-634dda28eed3" :activity/scope 17592186048877] ["activity-062eec2e-80dd-50ca-8380-634dda28eed3" :activity/course 17592186046658] ["activity-062eec2e-80dd-50ca-8380-634dda28eed3" :activity/steps "step-2842369f-1f4f-58be-953d-3f713b247f9b"] ["activity-062eec2e-80dd-50ca-8380-634dda28eed3" :activity/steps "step-6bba2763-669c-58c3-a3e6-5843dc68f94f"] ["activity-062eec2e-80dd-50ca-8380-634dda28eed3" :activity/steps "step-ad4fbff0-775d-5cc6-a06c-c04820df7660"] ["activity-062eec2e-80dd-50ca-8380-634dda28eed3" :activity/steps "step-a90d8bfb-8461-5668-8e11-2111cb68de7c"] ["activity-062eec2e-80dd-50ca-8380-634dda28eed3" :activity/steps "step-33af0b6f-ae95-578f-99a9-1e3131763eb2"] ["activity-062eec2e-80dd-50ca-8380-634dda28eed3" :activity/first-step "step-2842369f-1f4f-58be-953d-3f713b247f9b"]] "retractions" [[17592186323840 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2} & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}>0}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2} & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}>0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}>0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323338 :answer-field/correct 17592186322623] [17592186323338 :answer-field/choices 17592186322742] [17592186323338 :answer-field/choices 17592186321673] [17592186323338 :answer-field/choices 17592186321630] [17592186323338 :answer-field/choices 17592186322623] [17592186125193 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$ is defined only when $xy>0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}(x,y)=1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$ is defined only when $xy>0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${f}_{y}(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186335365 :answer-field/correct 17592186334552] [17592186335365 :answer-field/choices 17592186333364] [17592186335365 :answer-field/choices 17592186333924] [17592186335365 :answer-field/choices 17592186334552] [17592186335365 :answer-field/choices 17592186334888] [17592186335365 :answer-field/choices 17592186334591] [17592186339041 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z>0}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+y+z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{z}(x,y,z)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{z}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{z}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $z$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z>0}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z>0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338328 :answer-field/correct 17592186338151] [17592186338328 :answer-field/choices 17592186336837] [17592186338328 :answer-field/choices 17592186337733] [17592186338328 :answer-field/choices 17592186338151] [17592186338328 :answer-field/choices 17592186336201] [17592186338328 :answer-field/choices 17592186338122] [17592186381041 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & >0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,x+y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)}{x+y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+y}\\cdot \\frac{1}{z} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{z(x+y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{z}(x,y,z)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z(x+y) & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{z}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{z}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,x+y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $z$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,x+y\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,x+y\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,x+y\\ne 0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186381042 :answer-field/correct 17592186381045] [17592186381042 :answer-field/choices 17592186381043] [17592186381042 :answer-field/choices 17592186381044] [17592186381042 :answer-field/choices 17592186381045] [17592186381042 :answer-field/choices 17592186381046] [17592186381042 :answer-field/choices 17592186381047]]}]